          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state, while measuring it against her filial bond rather than using her sisters’ hyperbole.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes and enacts Cordelia’s existing filial obligation rather than making a new promise. Cordelia identifies herself as bound to return a daughter’s fitting duties—obedience, love, and honour—to her father.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The wording is a controlled avowal and invocation of duty, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty converting her inward feeling into speech, but the focal expression itself remains deliberate and measured.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a measured refusal to exaggerate for Lear’s competitive declaration of love. “No more nor less” limits the claim and implicitly resists the demanded performance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear asks his daughters to declare how much they love him while linking their answers to territorial rewards. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained declaration, contrasting with her sisters’ expansive professions.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes and enacts an existing daughterly bond: she owes Lear fitting love, obedience, care, and honour. She does not offer a new unlimited commitment and expressly reserves part of her future love and duty for a husband. Lear does not accept her measured formulation as satisfactory; he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt strongly supports Cordelia’s represented affection through her asides and speech, but it does not independently establish every term of the filial bond or developments beyond this scene.\"}"
        }
      ],
